<section aria-labelledby="token-audit-title">
	<h2 id="token-audit-title">04 / Design-token audit</h2>
	<p>
		Revision 8 source audit: shared <code>tokens.css</code>, shared
		<code>buttons.css</code>, and the study’s button examples. Values below
		describe the current implementation, including study overrides. The local
		spacing alias is now private; other token changes remain proposals. No
		visual values have changed.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>Backed</strong>
		means a reusable system token is used. <strong>Component-only</strong> means
		a token exists but its base value belongs only to buttons; this is not
		automatically a missing system token.
		<strong>Missing</strong>
		flags a hard-coded design choice.
		<strong>Mixed</strong>
		contains both tokens and literals.
		<strong>Domain-only</strong>
		borrows a flight-specific token.
		<strong>Structural</strong>
		describes layout mechanics;
		<strong>Fixture</strong>
		describes demonstration inputs.
	</p>
	<p>
		Token names proposed below are candidates for discussion, not accepted
		additions. Tokenize intentional visual values; keep flex/grid alignment,
		0/100% geometry, resets, pseudo-element layering, and native interaction
		semantics as CSS rules. A matching number is not sufficient reason to reuse
		an unrelated token. Values in rem are shown as authored, not assumed pixel
		equivalents.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>Private composition</strong>
		uses <code>--_name</code>, declared on the owning component rather than
		<code>:root</code>. Local aliases, derived values, and implementation
		details can stay private. Shared visual decisions remain public tokens. The
		promotion notes below distinguish the two; a private proposal is not a
		missing design-system token.
	</p>
	<p>
		For example, <code>--_group-spacing: var(--spacing-sm)</code> supplies both
		padding and gap. A button can privately alias system padding and radius, or
		derive its pseudo-element inset from a public border-width token. The naming
		convention documents ownership; CSS itself does not enforce privacy.
	</p>

